/**
 * @page architecture Architecture
 *
 * # Description of the layer model
 *
 * The architecture and the layered model are described below. The 1-Wire
 * interface consists of three layers:
 * 
 * -# OW::Hal is the hardware abstraction layer that decouples the library from
 *    the physical hardware. It is the driver module that must be adapted when
 *    porting the library to a different MCU platform.
 * -# OW::Bus – Provides the specific communication interface for the 1-Wire bus.
 *    It handles timing and low-level communication, thereby managing the
 *    transmission and reading of bit and byte values.
 * -# The device classes include OW::Device and the specific device classes.
 *    OW::Device contains general commands supported by all or multiple devices,
 *    while the specific device classes provide functions for a specific device
 *    (e.g., a DS18B20).
 *
 * ## Use of the individual layers or classes
 *
 * The individual classes build upon one another and must be configured
 * correctly in the proper sequence. This structure also has the advantage that
 * multiple buses can be operated on a single microcontroller. The maximum
 * number naturally depends on the processing power and the available inputs
 * and outputs.
 * 
 * ### Hal object
 *
 * An OW::Hal object must be created as the initial, lowest-level layer to
 * enable hardware control. It essentially acts as the driver for accessing
 * specific hardware. Please refer to the specific HAL class for further
 * details, as configuration may vary between hardware platforms.
 *
 * Here is an example of the HAL for an ATmega328P:
 * @code
 * OW::Hal myHal(&DDRD, &PIND, &PORTD, PORTD7);
 * @endcode
 * 
 * ### Bus object
 *
 * The OW::Bus class builds upon the OW::Hal class, utilizing the HAL as the
 * interface to the physical hardware and bus. It employs the HAL class's
 * functions to access specific hardware pins, timers, and registers. To do
 * this, the HAL object must be passed to the bus class so that the bus class
 * knows exactly which hardware interface it should use. In turn, the Bus
 * class provides fundamental communication functions to the device classes.
 *
 * This class can also be used to implement custom low-level communication with
 * 1-Wire devices, as it provides essential functions such as reset operations
 * and the transmission and reception of bits and bytes.
 *
 * After initializing the HAL, the bus instance can be created, as shown in the
 * following example:
 * @code
 * OW::Bus myInterface(myHal);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ### Device objects
 *
 * The device classes are created as the final layer. These classes are passed
 * the bus interface so that they know which bus interface to use to
 * communicate with the physical devices. They then provide device-specific
 * functions and data structures that can be used and evaluated within the
 * code.
 *
 * @note The specific device classes are optional, depending on which device is
 * to be used. However, the OW::Device class must always be instantiated, as it
 * provides general functions for all specific device classes.
 *
 * As an example, the required devices can be initialized as follows:
 * @code
 * OW::Device myDevice(myInterface);    // necessary
 * OW::Ds18B20 myDs18B20(myInterface);  // optional
 * OW::Ds2438 myDs2438(myInterface);    // optional
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Helper functions
 *
 * In addition to the 1-Wire core, further functions are provided. These
 * include functions for converting sensor data, as well as general-purpose
 * functions, given that 1-Wire sensors are frequently used in smart home
 * applications.
 */
